    estimatedMinutes: 12,
    prompt: "Reversal is three steps repeated: save the remaining suffix, flip the current link backward, then advance. At every step `previous` is the finished part and `current` starts what is left.\n\nReverse the list and return it. Build the result by moving one value at a time to the front of the finished part — do not use slicing or `reversed`.\n\nA linked list is given as a plain Python list of values, in order. Return a plain Python list — the point is the pointer reasoning, not the node class.",
